// M1 — Marketing and generative AI — Session 2. Choosing a launch positioning
$CATEGORY: Master/Marketing/2. Positioning

::Frame of reference::In a positioning statement, what is the frame of reference?{=The category in which the product competes ~The launch price ~The advertising slogan ~The age range of the target}

::Point of difference::A strong point of difference should be…{=desirable for the target, deliverable by the brand and distinctive from competitors ~shared with all competitors in the category ~chosen by the advertising agency ~expressed only through the price}

::Points of parity::What are points of parity?{=Attributes the brand must share with competitors to be credible in its category ~Attributes that no competitor can copy ~Discounts matched to competitors' prices ~The brand's market share compared with the leader}

::When everyone recycles::If most competitors also sell running shoes made from recycled materials, "made from recycled materials" becomes…{=a point of parity ~a point of difference ~a frame of reference ~a reason to believe that cannot be copied}

::Reason to believe::In a positioning statement, the reason to believe is…{=the evidence that makes the promised benefit credible ~the reason why the company launches the product ~the target's main motivation to run ~the emotional tone of the campaign}

::Perceptual map::What does a perceptual map show?{=How consumers perceive brands along dimensions that matter to them ~Where the shops are located ~How sales evolve month by month ~The organisation chart of the marketing team}
